{"seconds": 4.595546916825697, "submission_file": "experiments/generation-extension-v1/submissions/H01/gpt-4.1-2025-04-14.json", "submission_sha256": "c8bf2ca6ea643e1d9dce7b89512e46b44c2ed41e5484500388dfa673832fbfd1", "code_sha256": "968061e98aa19cbb8017875ee3edc6525c2051d42f1c037c674144bc28f79710", "description": "This solution implements a classical EEG epoch decoding approach using time-domain feature extraction suitable for sensor-space EEG with physically-referenced electrodes and minimal prior information. Inspired by common spatial pattern (CSP)-like approaches but without specialist libraries, the pipeline extracts log-variance features from manually-selected central-motor electrodes known to respond to hand motor imagery (C3, C4, CZ). For each trial, the log-variance is computed from short, overlapping windows in these channels. Additionally, mean, standard deviation, and band power features in the mu (8-13 Hz) and beta (13-30 Hz) bands are computed for all channels using SciPy's FFT. Channels are processed independently and concatenated to form a compact but rich trial feature vector. All feature extraction uses only training data to avoid leakage. A logistic regression classifier is trained using balanced class weights for robustness against potential label imbalance, and Scikit-learn's StratifiedKFold cross-validation is used to select the regularization strength in a leave-one-subject-out (LOSO) fashion (subject IDs are not provided, so LOSO is approximated with 5-fold stratified splits). No data from test subjects is used in any training step. The final model predicts the test set in a single pass. All NumPy/SciPy/sklearn operations use deterministic seeds; no external packages are used. The approach is designed for balanced accuracy scoring and robust, generalizable performance across held-out test subjects.", "code_lines": 95, "line_limit_compliant": true, "line_limit_exclusive": 240}

